ter  of a single mother things go messy and  the young girl is believed drowned  by the end it's learned that she is in  fact alive and in the care of a police  captain and his wife  who staged the kidnapping to get the  girl away from her neglectful mother  this leaves patrick with one of two  choices  leave a young girl in the care of a  loving couple of means  and let her actual mother believe her to  be dead  or report the whole thing bring  kidnappers to justice  and return a four-year-old to a broken  home where she's barely cared for  when the lights go out and you ask  yourself is she better off here or  better off there  you know the answer what's never gonna  happen and what i'm not gonna do  is have to apologize to a grown woman  who comes to me and says i was kidnapped  when i was a little girl  and my aunt hired you to find me and you  did you found me with some strange  family  but you broke your promise and you left  me there patrick chooses the latter  in direct opposition of what his  girlfriend thinks he should do so he  loses that relationship  the captain is arrested and the girl is  returned to her mother  when i was watching this i kept waiting  for the situation to become less morally  messy i was expecting one of a few  things maybe it would turn out  the captain was actually a child  molester was planning to later traffic  the girl  or he never was gonna just raise her  safely  or maybe once uh we get her back  into the custody of her mother we get a  reassuring scene  of the mother being more attentive and  making it seem like nearly losing her  daughter and china she needs to do  better and the audience could at least  hope for improvement on the home front  neither of these things nor any kind of  equivalent  ends up happening the girl is returned  patrick goes to visit the mother who is  on her way out for a date even though no  babysitter has turned up yet  patrick offers to stay with the child  and we find out that the mother  still doesn't even know the name of her  kids favorite doll  the moral mess of kidnapping is wrong  but it would have been an improved life  is left exactly  as is there is no moment of moral  clarity where it becomes crystal clear  what the right choice was  not for the characters and not for the  audience and it has  stuck with me ever since and i'll be  blunt it's not pleasant  it's a haunting experience it's not  uplifting it's not triumphant and it  sure as hell isn't cathartic  which is of course why it happens so  rarely don't misunderstand me on this  there's nothing  wrong with rooting for definitely good  guys to beat up  definitely bad guys but i can't help but  get a bit antsy  when films and stories and shows  introduce  some degree of moral complexity but then  never properly grapple with it  aside from a general sense of lost  potential  there are other issues to consider as  well the entertainment we take in  affects us not in a clumsy one to one i  saw that in a movie so now i believe it  kind of way but  rather in a marinating in the culture  kind of way  and if nearly every time a villain has  more complex moral questions behind him  the film ultimately removes that  complexity by the end  then the films are telling audiences  things may seem complicated but don't  worry  it's actually quite simple you can trust  that the designated bad guys are  definitely bad  and if villains have motives that appear  to be applicable to real world causes  and by the end it's revealed that they  are definitely going too far  or that the cause is just a front for  something nefarious  it's telling the audience to question  what might appear to be  reasonable motives now i need to stress  uh this is almost never deliberate it's  a natural consequence of creators  wanting to have their story not be 100  black and white  but also not wanting to give up the  catharsis of the heroes beating villains  but the thing to remember is that when  we lack real life experience  we're 